   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law and the general municipal law, in relation\nto authorizing industrial development agencies to finance certain facilities\nfor the small city school districts",
    "basePrintNo": "S6795",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6795-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2503, Ed L; amd §858, Gen Muni L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6795\n\nSPONSOR: MAYER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law and the general municipal law, in\nrelation to authorizing industrial development agencies to finance\ncertain facilities for the small city school districts\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo permit small city school districts to finance capital projects\nthrough industrial development agencies and authorities.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSections one and two amend the education law and the general municipal\nlaw to include small city school districts for purposes of financing\ncapital projects through industrial development agencies.\n\nSection two further states such financings shall be deemed state\ncontracts under the executive law and allows for lease, sublease or\nother agreements between the entities and the small city school\ndistricts.\n\nSection 3 establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNon-city, fiscally independent school districts in New York State are\nsubject to a 10 percent debt limit pursuant to the provisions of the\nLocal Finance Law and may deduct Building Aid from the debt ceiling\ncomputation. Small city school districts, however, are limited to a five\npercent debt limit and are unable to apply Building Aid to offset this\ncap. The restriction on debt capacity for small city school districts\nhas limited construction, reconstruction, rehabilitation and moderniza-\ntion of their school buildings and is ultimately contributing to more\ncostly projects over longer periods of time. Additionally, efforts to\nimprove learning environments and maintain appropriate classroom\nsettings are impacted by borrowing limitations. This bill would align\nwith measures already adopted for large city school district to enable\nsmall city school districts to advance necessary school construction,\nreconstruction and renovation projects by permitting an alternate\nfinancing mechanism. Voter approval for such projects would continue to\nbe required.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone in 2021-22. Building Aid reimbursement is lagged and paid on a debt\nservice schedule payable over multiple years.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
